s over a field. Singular curves are treated through a detailed study of the Picard group. The second part starts with blowing-ups and desingularisation (embedded or not) of fibered surfaces over a Dedekind ring that leads on to intersection theory on arithmetic surfaces. Castelnuovo's criterion is proved and also the existence of the minimal regular model. This leads to the study of reduction of algebraic curves. The case of elliptic curves is studied in detail. The book concludes with the funadmental theorem of stable reduction of Deligne-Mumford. The book is essentially self-contained, including the necessary material on commutative algebra. The prerequisites are therefore few, and the book should suit a graduate student. It contains many examples and nearly 600 exercises.\n\nContents\n\n  * ### Front Matter\n\n    * Title Page\n    * Dedication\n    * Preface\n    * Contents\n  * 1 Some topics incommutative algebra\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 2 General properties of schemes\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 3 Morphisms and base change\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 4 Some local properties\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 5 Coherent sheaves and Cech cohomology\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 6 Sheaves of differentials\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 7 Divisors and applications to curves\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 8 Birational geometry of surfaces\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 9 Regular surfaces\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 10 Reduction of algebraic curves\n\nView chapter\n\n  * ### End Matter\n\n    * Bibliography\n    * Index\n\n© Oxford University Press 2002--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThermodynamics and an Introduction to Thermostatistics, 2nd Edition | Wiley (https://uat.store.wiley.com/en-us/thermodynamics-and-an-introduction-to-thermostatistics-2nd-edition-p-9780471862567)\nciteturn383search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Herbert Bernard Callen was an American physicist best known as the author of the textbook Thermodynamics and an Introduction to Thermostatistics,